<!-- FLIGHT THEORY -->
<section class="section" id="theory">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2>📘 Flight Theory Academy</h2>
    <p>Learn the science behind flight, from aerodynamics to navigation.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="theory-grid">
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>🛫 Four Forces of Flight</h3>
      <p><b>Lift:</b> aerodynamic force that acts generally upward relative to the aircraft.</p>
      <p><b>Weight:</b> the force of gravity acting on the aircraft.</p>
      <p><b>Thrust:</b> force produced by the propulsion system.</p>
      <p><b>Drag:</b> aerodynamic resistance opposing motion through the air.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>🌬️ How Wings Create Lift</h3>
      <p>Lift depends on air density, airspeed, wing area and lift coefficient.
      Wing shape and angle of attack affect airflow and pressure distribution.</p>
      <div class="note">Lift equation: L = ½ ρ V² S Cₗ</div>
      <p class="small">Here, ρ is air density, V is airspeed, S is wing area and Cₗ is lift coefficient.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>⚠️ Stalls and Angle of Attack</h3>
      <p>A stall occurs when a wing exceeds its critical angle of attack.
      It can occur at many airspeeds, not only at a particular low speed.</p>
      <p>Stall awareness and recovery procedures depend on aircraft type and approved training.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>🎮 Flight Controls</h3>
      <p><b>Ailerons:</b> primarily control roll.</p>
      <p><b>Elevator:</b> controls pitch in conventional designs.</p>
      <p><b>Rudder:</b> controls yaw.</p>
      <p><b>Flaps:</b> increase lift and drag for take-off and landing configurations.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>⚙️ Engines and Propulsion</h3>
      <p>Turbofans are common on airliners. Turboprops drive a propeller using a turbine.
      Piston engines are common in light aircraft, while rockets carry their own oxidizer.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>🌦️ Weather and Navigation</h3>
      <p>Pilots consider wind, visibility, clouds, turbulence, icing and storms.
      Navigation may use GNSS, radio aids, inertial systems, charts and approved procedures.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>⚖️ Weight and Balance</h3>
      <p>Aircraft mass and centre of gravity affect stability, control and performance.
      Operators calculate loading within approved limits.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>🛩️ Stability and Trim</h3>
      <p>Stability describes how an aircraft responds after a disturbance.
      Trim reduces the sustained control force needed in a particular flight condition.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="theory-card">
      <h3>📟 Flight Instruments</h3>
      <p>Airspeed, altitude, attitude, heading and vertical speed are important
      indications. Modern aircraft may integrate them into electronic displays.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

